```java
public List<BlockNode> getCleanSuccessors() {
    return this.cleanSuccessors;
}
```

So nullability depends entirely on whether `this.cleanSuccessors` can be null for the specific `BlockNode` instance referenced by `block`.

From the provided call chain and surrounding usages:

- Many call sites explicitly assume non-null and only guard for emptiness or size, e.g.:

  ```java
  for (BlockNode node : block.getCleanSuccessors()) { ... }          // ordinary foreach
  if (block.getCleanSuccessors().size() == 1) { ... }
  List<BlockNode> successors = block.getCleanSuccessors();
  if (successors.size() != 1) { ... }
  return s.isEmpty() ? null : s.get(0);  // where s = block.getCleanSuccessors()
