  These sites do not perform a null check on the list itself; instead they rely on it being a valid (possibly empty) `List`.

- Where the authors were concerned about potential nullability, they wrapped the call in `Nullability.castToNonnull(...)`, for example:

  ```java
  BlockNode block = Nullability.castToNonnull(mth.getEnterBlock().getCleanSuccessors()).get(0);
  ...
  for (BlockNode s : Nullability.castToNonnull(block.getCleanSuccessors())) { ... }
  ...
  if (Nullability.castToNonnull(block.getCleanSuccessors()).size() >= 2) { ... }
  ...
  while (currentBlock != null
         && Nullability.castToNonnull(currentBlock.getCleanSuccessors()).size() < 2
         ...
  ```

  This pattern appears in utility contexts intended to be generally safe even if some `BlockNode` has a null `cleanSuccessors` (e.g., defensive coding in utilities).

- In contrast, in core CFG-building and traversal logic (e.g. `BlockUtils.getNextBlock`, `isEmptySimplePath`, `isPathExists`, `RegionMaker.traverse`, `RegionMaker.makeLoopRegion`, `MarkFinallyVisitor.getSuccessorsWithoutLoop`, `IfMakerHelper.checkConditionBranches`), `getCleanSuccessors()` is used directly with no null-guard. These are operating on “normal” blocks in a fully constructed CFG where `cleanSuccessors` is expected to be initialized as at least an empty list.

- `BlockExceptionHandler.wrapBlocksWithTryCatch` ends with:

  ```java
  topSplitterBlock.updateCleanSuccessors();
  if (bottomSplitterBlock != null) {
      bottomSplitterBlock.updateCleanSuccessors();
  }
